Your softmax is normalizing over the wrong axis. x_train has shape (60000, 784), so predict(x_train) produces Z2 with shape (60000, 10): one row per sample, one column per class. With axis=0, np.max(x) is a single global scalar and e_x.sum(axis=0) sums down the 60000 samples for each class column, not across the 10 classes within each sample. So a row of A2 is not a probability distribution at all; only each column sums to 1 across samples, which is why sum(output[0]) came out near zero. Fix by normalizing along the last axis (per row):

def softmax(x):
    e_x = np.exp(x - np.max(x, axis=-1, keepdims=True))
    return e_x / e_x.sum(axis=-1, keepdims=True)

Separately, your weights are unscaled randn, which can produce very large logits and cause overflow/underflow inside exp; scale them down (e.g. * 0.01) or use He/Xavier initialization for more stable training.

This is expected behavior, not a bug: asyncio.gather() propagates the first exception to the caller, but it does not cancel the other awaitables - they keep running in the background unless you cancel them yourself. The docs say this explicitly: "other awaitables in the aws sequence won't be cancelled and will continue to run." Fix by keeping references to the tasks and cancelling them yourself on failure:

tasks = [asyncio.create_task(worker(i)) for i in range(5)]
try:
    await asyncio.gather(*tasks)
except Exception as e:
    print("Error:", e)
    for t in tasks:
        t.cancel()
    await asyncio.gather(*tasks, return_exceptions=True)

On Python 3.11+, asyncio.TaskGroup is the cleaner alternative: if one child task raises, the group automatically cancels all sibling tasks and raises an ExceptionGroup when its context manager exits - that "cancel siblings on first failure" behavior is exactly what gather() intentionally does not provide.

Pure regex can't fully parse arbitrary strings, but it can reliably tell "// inside a string" apart from "// starting a comment" if you match strings and comments as alternatives in one pass, always letting a full string literal win first:

import re
pattern = re.compile(r'"(?:\\.|[^"\\])*"|//.*$', re.MULTILINE)
def strip_comments(text):
    return pattern.sub(lambda m: m.group(0) if m.group(0).startswith('"') else "", text)

Because the string alternative is tried at each position and, when it matches, consumes the whole quoted string (including any // inside it, like your URLs) in one match, the // comment alternative never gets a chance to fire there - it only matches an actual // outside any string. This is the standard trick for this exact problem, and works as long as your JSON strings only use normal backslash escaping (no unbalanced quotes). Note your example also has trailing commas, which strict JSON doesn't allow - strip those separately afterward with re.sub(r',(\s*[}\]])', r'\1', cleaned).
